title: Baptist Groups Compared
description: Independent Fundamental Baptist, Southern Baptist, Reformed or Particular, Landmark and Free Will or Arminian Baptists compared on the gospel, the Baptist distinctives, church order, Scripture and separation, with the IFB position as the baseline.
output: lessons/history/baptist-groups-compared.html

# Rebuilt from the original page. Each comparison card becomes a table slide with one row per group (a five-column
# chart does not fit at phone width). The original page had no SVG chart; its chart was the five colour-coded group blocks.
# The comparisons are the author's evaluation from an IFB / KJVO baseline and are kept as written.

=== Introduction
--- Baptist Groups Compared [title]
overline: Adult Sunday School
heading: Baptist Groups Compared
sub: An IFB / KJVO baseline
hint: Use the arrow keys or swipe to move. Tap any reference to read the verse.

--- The five groups
lead: Prepared for IFB / KJVO adult Sunday school use. The Independent Fundamental Baptist position is presented as the baseline; other groups are evaluated by that standard.
- IFB baseline
- Southern Baptist
- Reformed / Particular
- Landmark
- Free Will / Arminian
note: All Scripture quotations, Authorized (King James) Version.

=== 1. Core gospel foundation
--- Core gospel foundation
kicker: Foundation
lead: This chart treats classic Independent Fundamental Baptist doctrine as the closest practical match to New Testament church order and gospel preaching. These four points are non-negotiable. Variations that subtract from or add conditions to them are treated as serious error.
panel: Grace through faith alone | No front-loaded or back-loaded works. Faith is the empty hand that receives Christ. [[Ephesians 2:8-9]]
panel: Provisional / general atonement | Christ died for the whole world. The invitation is genuinely “whosoever will.” [[John 3:16]]
panel: Once Saved Always Saved | The believer is sealed and kept by God. Apostasy proves a person was never saved. [[John 10:28-29]]
panel: Human responsibility | Man is genuinely responsible and able to respond in free response to the gospel call. [[Romans 10:13]]
callout: Reformed Calvinism and Free-Will Arminianism are viewed as two sides of the same coin. Both move away from this simple, provisional gospel center.

=== 2. Baptist distinctives
--- The BAPTISTS acrostic
kicker: Baptist distinctives
lead: Most historic Baptists formally affirm the BAPTISTS acrostic. Differences appear in application and in the doctrines underneath.
panel: B · Biblical Authority | Scripture alone is final authority. No creed or confession may bind the conscience above the text. Preservation implies a pure English text (KJV).
panel-warn: Common drift | Confessionalism elevates 1689/Westminster language above plain reading. Multiple versions undermine preservation.
panel: A · Autonomy of the Local Church | Each local assembly is independent under Christ. No hierarchy, convention, or association has authority over the church.
panel-warn: Common drift | SBC Cooperative Program and association pressures. Some Reformed networks function semi-presbyterially.

--- Priesthood, ordinances and soul liberty
kicker: Baptist distinctives
panel: P · Priesthood of the Believer | Every believer has direct access to God and the right/responsibility to interpret Scripture under the Spirit.
panel-warn: Common drift | Heavy confessional gate-keeping or elder-rule systems can functionally limit this.
panel: T · Two Ordinances | Baptism (immersion of believers) and the Lord’s Supper only. Neither is salvific.
panel-warn: Common drift | Some Free Will / Separate groups add foot-washing as a third ordinance.
panel: I · Individual Soul Liberty | No civil or ecclesiastical power may coerce conscience in matters of faith.
panel-warn: Common drift | Generally retained, though some Landmark or confessional circles tighten practical application.

--- Membership, offices and security
kicker: Baptist distinctives
panel: S · Saved Church Membership | Only those who give credible evidence of regeneration and believer’s baptism are received.
panel-warn: Common drift | Practically weakened where open membership or weak discipline exists.
panel: T · Two Church Offices | Pastor (bishop/elder) and deacons. The pastor is under-shepherd; plurality of equal elders is not required by the text.
panel-warn: Common drift | Reformed Baptists often insist on plurality of elders as normative.
panel: S · Security of the Believer | Eternal security / OSAS. The truly saved are kept by the power of God. Apostasy proves the person was never saved.
panel-warn: Common drift | Arminian groups teach conditional security. Some Calvinists reframe it as perseverance that can look like works.

--- Confession or Scripture
kicker: Baptist distinctives
callout: Holding a detailed confession (e.g., the 1689 London Baptist Confession) as binding tends to drift from pure biblical authority. The confession becomes a lens that can override the plain reading of individual texts.

=== 3. Soteriology
--- Soteriology: the gospel core
kicker: Soteriology
lead: Grace through faith plus OSAS is the foundation. Systems that condition salvation on human performance (Arminian) or restrict the gospel offer and redefine security in terms of perseverance works (Calvinist) both miss the mark.
note: The next six slides compare the five groups, one question at a time.

--- Salvation by faith alone
kicker: Soteriology
table: Group | Position
tr: **IFB baseline** | By grace through faith, not of works ([[Ephesians 2:8-9]]). No Lordship front-loading.
tr: **Southern Baptist** | Generally affirms sola fide. Growing Calvinist minority introduces nuances.
tr: **Reformed / Particular** | Affirms justification by faith alone, yet ties true faith so tightly to perseverance that assurance is disturbed.
tr: **Landmark** | Generally holds faith alone; some circles emphasize local-church baptism heavily.
tr: **Free Will / Arminian** | Often mixes faith with ongoing obedience or the possibility of falling away, effectively adding works.

--- Atonement scope
kicker: Soteriology
table: Group | Position
tr: **IFB baseline** | General / provisional. Christ died for the world ([[John 3:16]]; [[1 John 2:2]]; [[1 Timothy 2:4-6]]).
tr: **Southern Baptist** | Majority Traditionalist/Provisionist view is general atonement. Calvinist minority holds limited atonement.
tr: **Reformed / Particular** | Limited / particular atonement. Christ died only for the elect. This restricts the free offer of the gospel.
tr: **Landmark** | Usually general atonement, consistent with IFB on this point.
tr: **Free Will / Arminian** | General atonement, but paired with conditional security.

--- Election and free will
kicker: Soteriology
table: Group | Position
tr: **IFB baseline** | God elects according to His foreknowledge; man is genuinely responsible and able to respond ([[Romans 10:13]]).
tr: **Southern Baptist** | Mixed. Many hold a soft view of election; growing 5-point minority. Most still preach “whosoever will.”
tr: **Reformed / Particular** | Unconditional election and irresistible grace. The free offer is effectively only for the elect.
tr: **Landmark** | Usually closer to IFB: responsibility and free response emphasized.
tr: **Free Will / Arminian** | Strong free-will emphasis, often at the expense of God’s sovereignty. Salvation becomes man-centered.

--- Eternal security (OSAS)
kicker: Soteriology
table: Group | Position
tr: **IFB baseline** | Once saved, always saved. Sealed by the Spirit ([[Ephesians 1:13-14]]; [[John 10:28-29]]).
tr: **Southern Baptist** | Officially affirms eternal security. Vast majority of churches teach OSAS.
tr: **Reformed / Particular** | Perseverance of the saints. The elect will persevere; those who fall away were never elect. Can functionally resemble works-based assurance.
tr: **Landmark** | Generally holds eternal security / OSAS, consistent with IFB.
tr: **Free Will / Arminian** | Conditional security. A believer can lose salvation through willful sin or apostasy. Treated here as a grave error.

--- Apostasy passages
kicker: Soteriology
table: Group | Position
tr: **IFB baseline** | Hebrews 6, 10, etc. describe those who tasted but were never regenerated. “They went out because they were not of us” ([[1 John 2:19]]).
tr: **Southern Baptist** | Most interpret consistently with eternal security. Some softer voices exist.
tr: **Reformed / Particular** | Usually interpreted as evidence of non-election. The warning passages function as means God uses to keep the elect.
tr: **Landmark** | Generally the same as IFB.
tr: **Free Will / Arminian** | Taken as proof that true believers can fall from grace and be lost. Misreads the texts.

--- Works and assurance
kicker: Soteriology
table: Group | Position
tr: **IFB baseline** | Good works follow salvation but never secure or prove it. Assurance rests on Christ’s finished work and the Spirit’s witness.
tr: **Southern Baptist** | Generally similar. Some Calvinist circles push Lordship or fruit-inspection that unsettles weak believers.
tr: **Reformed / Particular** | Strong emphasis on fruit and perseverance as evidence. Can produce a practical works-righteousness of introspection.
tr: **Landmark** | Usually closer to IFB simplicity.
tr: **Free Will / Arminian** | Ongoing obedience is often treated as a condition of remaining saved.

=== 4. Ecclesiology, Scripture and separation
--- Ecclesiology, Scripture and separation
kicker: Ecclesiology
lead: A New Testament church is local, autonomous, evangelistic, and under the pure preserved Word.
note: The next eight slides compare the five groups, one question at a time.

--- Local church autonomy
kicker: Ecclesiology
table: Group | Position
tr: **IFB baseline** | Absolute under Christ. No convention, board, or association has authority over the local assembly. Fellowship is voluntary.
tr: **Southern Baptist** | Officially autonomous, yet the Cooperative Program and convention structures create practical interdependence and pressure.
tr: **Reformed / Particular** | Autonomous in theory. Many practice elder-rule and association accountability that can limit congregational freedom.
tr: **Landmark** | Very strong emphasis on local-church authority, often the strongest among Baptist groups.
tr: **Free Will / Arminian** | Generally autonomous; associations exist but the local church remains primary.

--- Succession and Landmarkism
kicker: Ecclesiology
table: Group | Position
tr: **IFB baseline** | Many IFB hold a practical Baptist succession or “trail of blood” view, emphasizing continuity of New Testament order rather than an unbroken institutional chain.
tr: **Southern Baptist** | Generally rejects strict Landmark successionism. Accepts broader evangelical baptism in many cases.
tr: **Reformed / Particular** | Rejects Landmark succession. Sees continuity through doctrine and the true church.
tr: **Landmark** | Core distinctive: only Baptist churches (in succession) are true churches. Closed communion; rejects “alien immersion.”
tr: **Free Will / Arminian** | Usually does not hold Landmark succession.

--- Communion
kicker: Ecclesiology
table: Group | Position
tr: **IFB baseline** | Typically close or closed: local-church members or those of like faith and order. Not open to the unbaptized or those in known error.
tr: **Southern Baptist** | Varies widely. Open, close, and closed all exist. Trend toward more open practice.
tr: **Reformed / Particular** | Often close (baptized believers of like faith). Some stricter.
tr: **Landmark** | Closed communion, restricted to members of the local church (or churches of like faith and order).
tr: **Free Will / Arminian** | Varies; many practice open or close communion.

--- Offices
kicker: Ecclesiology
table: Group | Position
tr: **IFB baseline** | Pastor (bishop/elder) and deacons. One primary teaching pastor is the New Testament pattern; plurality is permitted but not required.
tr: **Southern Baptist** | Pastor and deacons standard. Some churches move toward elder plurality.
tr: **Reformed / Particular** | Strong preference for plurality of elders as the normative model.
tr: **Landmark** | Usually pastor and deacons, consistent with historic Baptist practice.
tr: **Free Will / Arminian** | Pastor and deacons; some groups have additional practices.

--- Scripture and preservation
kicker: Ecclesiology
table: Group | Position
tr: **IFB baseline** | Verbal plenary inspiration + providential preservation. In English this leads to the King James Version. Modern critical-text versions are rejected.
tr: **Southern Baptist** | Inerrancy officially affirmed. Majority use modern versions (ESV, NIV, CSB); KJV preference exists but is minority.
tr: **Reformed / Particular** | Inerrancy held. Almost universally use modern translations based on the critical text. Confessions treated as highly authoritative summaries.
tr: **Landmark** | Many Landmark churches are KJV-preferred or KJVO, similar to IFB.
tr: **Free Will / Arminian** | Generally accept modern versions; some older groups retain KJV.

--- Evangelism and missions
kicker: Ecclesiology
table: Group | Position
tr: **IFB baseline** | Essential mark of a NT church. Aggressive soul-winning, local evangelism, and faith-mission support of church planting ([[Acts 1:8]]; [[Matthew 28:18-20]]).
tr: **Southern Baptist** | Strong missions emphasis through the Cooperative Program and IMB. Methods and theological breadth vary.
tr: **Reformed / Particular** | Missions affirmed, but limited atonement and emphasis on the elect can dampen aggressive “whosoever” evangelism in practice.
tr: **Landmark** | Strong missions emphasis in many Landmark/Missionary Baptist groups, often through their own associations.
tr: **Free Will / Arminian** | Evangelism affirmed; conditional security can affect how the gospel is presented and followed up.

--- Ecclesiastical separation
kicker: Ecclesiology
table: Group | Position
tr: **IFB baseline** | Clear separation from apostasy, liberalism, and neo-evangelical compromise ([[2 Corinthians 6:14-18]]; [[2 Thessalonians 3:6]]; [[2 Thessalonians 3:14]]). First- and second-degree separation practiced.
tr: **Southern Baptist** | Officially conservative since the Conservative Resurgence, yet remains in cooperative structures including broader evangelicalism. Separation generally weaker.
tr: **Reformed / Particular** | Doctrinal separation on Calvinistic distinctives is often strong; fundamentalist-style separation from neo-evangelicalism is less consistent.
tr: **Landmark** | Often strong separation, especially regarding baptism and church order.
tr: **Free Will / Arminian** | Varies widely; many are less separatist.

--- Confessionalism
kicker: Ecclesiology
table: Group | Position
tr: **IFB baseline** | No binding creed beyond Scripture. Statements of faith are descriptive, not magisterial. Elevating a confession drifts from sola Scriptura.
tr: **Southern Baptist** | Baptist Faith & Message is a consensus document, not a rigid confession. Churches may differ within limits.
tr: **Reformed / Particular** | Highly confessional (1689 London Confession or similar). The confession functions as a secondary authority and interpretive grid.
tr: **Landmark** | Often use historic Baptist confessions or abstracts, but emphasis remains on local church and Scripture.
tr: **Free Will / Arminian** | Some have treatises or confessions; less rigid than Reformed Baptists.

=== 5. Where others miss the mark
--- Where others miss the mark
kicker: Summary
point: The gospel center must not be moved | Reformed Calvinism (by limiting the atonement and reframing security around inevitable perseverance) and Arminian Free-Will systems (by making security conditional) both move the gospel off center. This affects how the gospel is preached, how assurance is given, and how the church understands its mission.
point: Biblical authority vs. confessional drift | When a detailed confession becomes the functional authority or required interpretive grid, the church has begun to drift from sola Scriptura. Historic Baptist practice treated confessions as useful summaries, not binding secondary standards.
point: Preservation and the English Bible | If God inspired His Word and promised to preserve it, then in the English-speaking world that preserved Word is found in the King James Version. Churches that treat modern critical-text versions as equal or superior have accepted a different doctrine of preservation.
point: Evangelism and missions are not optional | A New Testament church is a soul-winning, missionary-sending body. Any system that dampens the free offer of the gospel or shifts energy away from aggressive evangelism has missed a central mark of the early church.
point: Separation protects the testimony | Ecclesiastical and personal separation are obedience to clear commands, not mean-spiritedness. Cooperation with unbelief or persistent doctrinal error eventually erodes the gospel and the purity of the church.
note: Prepared for IFB / KJVO adult Sunday school use. The Independent Fundamental Baptist position is presented as the baseline; other groups are evaluated by that standard.
